  "worked_solution": "Let's check each statement:\n\n- To determine whether ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute ${y}_{1}$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2033}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(-4\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x)+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x=(4{x}^{3}-4x)\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+2\\operatorname{sin}\u20612x.\\,\\times \n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is false.\n\n- Similarly, we compute the derivatives of ${y}_{2}$ and substitute to the equation. Computing the derivatives yields\n\nFirst, we find the first and second derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}(x) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033}(x) & =2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation. We get\n\n$$\nx(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{x}^{2}-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{x}^{2})-2x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{x}^{2}=(2x-2x)\\operatorname{cos}\u2061{x}^{2}+(4{x}^{3}-4{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{x}^{2}=0.\\,\u2713\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution to the equation, and therefore statement II is true.\n\n- So, ${y}_{2}$ is a solution while ${y}_{1}$ is not a solution of the equation, hence $y=A\\operatorname{cos}\u20612x+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061{x}^{2}$  is not the general solution.\n\nTherefore, statement III is false.",
